From f6f2c4b549b544852a8ebb912f8df699954af181 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dario Gabriel Lipicar <lipigl@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 30 Jul 2026 10:32:53 -0300
Subject: [PATCH 5/5] Emit enums into the meta object
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Enums were absent from the meta object entirely: the enumerator section was
never written, so `QMetaObject::enumerator()` reported nothing and a nimside
object could not describe a `Q_ENUM` to QML, to a QtRemoteObjects replica, or to
anything else reading the meta object.

Add `{.qenums(A, B).}` / `{.qflags(...).}` on the type, naming enums declared in
the same `qobject:` block -- they have to be declared there, since the block is
macro input and their keys are only readable from the AST in hand.

Three details are load-bearing:

  * Each enumerator needs a slot in the metaTypes array, right after the
    properties. QMetaEnum::metaType() reads metaTypes[index + propertyCount]
    unconditionally -- the `revision < 12` guard ahead of it is missing its
    `return` -- and QMetaEnum::value() reaches it through enumExtendMode() for
    any enum that is neither a flag nor 64-bit. Without the slots that is a read
    past the end of the array.

  * Enum-typed properties and parameters are recorded as `int`. Recording the
    enum's own name leaves the meta type unresolved, since Qt has no registered
    type for a Nim enum, and an unresolved meta type cannot be read or written
    through the meta object at all -- the value simply never propagates. As
    `int` it marshals, and a consumer generated from the same interface declares
    the property with its enum type and converts on arrival.

  * Values cross the boundary through int32 explicitly rather than by memcpy of
    sizeof(T): Nim sizes an enum to the smallest type its values fit in, so the
    obvious memcpy reads one byte of a four-byte slot.

EnumOrFlag is deliberately not set on such properties: Qt only resolves it when
the meta type also reports IsEnumeration, which needs a registered enum type
that nimside cannot synthesise, so claiming it would assert something Qt then
ignores.

Also fixes findPragma, which only ever examined the first entry in a pragma
block -- `{.slot, raises: [].}`, straight out of the README, never registered a
slot, and `{.qclassinfo(…), qenums(…).}` silently lost the second pragma.

tests/test_all: 64 OK.
